WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.160
Every TV show has to come to an end eventually.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.569
Some end of the creators' own accord.

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:09.400
Even more are canceled by the network that
airs them.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.790
Here are some of the saddest reasons ever
that TV shows were forced to end their runs.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:21.200
COVID-19 put a nearly uniform stop to most
of show business, along with the rest of the

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.200
world.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.920
Almost every TV and movie production came
to a screeching halt.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.130
Many projects got pushed back, and at least
one was canceled mid-production: Netflix wrestling

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.879
dramedy "GLOW."

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:38.219
Even though it had several unresolved storylines,
the show was canceled because of the unique

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:39.870
difficulties it presented.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:45.539
"GLOW" was three weeks into filming its fourth
season when the world changed in March 2020.

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:50.559
Netflix spent the next seven months trying
to find a safe way to restart production,

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:53.920
but the particular circumstances were too
difficult to overcome.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.199
The show was shot in Los Angeles, a COVID
hotspot in and of itself.

00:00:58.199 --> 00:01:03.129
Even worse, the show's core was wrestling
— an activity that requires physical contact

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:05.670
and heavy breathing in close quarters.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.189
Combine all this with the show being a high
budget ensemble piece with about 20 principal

00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:14.530
cast members, and Netflix just couldn't find
a way to make it work safely.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.710
Deadline broke the inevitable news on October
5, 2020: Netflix had canceled "GLOW."

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.670
Series creators Liz Flahive and Carly Mensch
shared,

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.630
"COVID has killed actual humans.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.590
It's a national tragedy and should be our
focus.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.479
COVID also apparently took down our show."

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:34.689
Thankfully, there are no reports of anyone
involved in the production catching COVID,

00:01:34.689 --> 00:01:37.450
and all the series regulars were paid in full.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.049
The cast has since expressed interest in a
feature length movie to wrap up the story.

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:47.990
Robert Schimmel's life was more tragedy than
comedy, up to and including his 2010 death

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.259
in a car accident.

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:53.450
One such tragedy was the story surrounding
his planned sitcom, "Schimmel" — and how

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.789
it never got past the pilot.

00:01:55.789 --> 00:01:59.749
In 1999, Schimmel signed on to do a sitcom
with 20th Century Fox.

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:03.909
Mike Scully, executive producer of "The Simpsons,"
was set to have a hand in it.

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.960
A pilot was shot — in which a very young
Brie Larson played his daughter — and got

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.660
picked up by the Fox network in 2000.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.710
Mere days after the announcement, however,
Schimmel was diagnosed with non-Hodgkin's

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.830
lymphoma and given six months to live.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.220
Production on his sitcom stopped while he
went into treatment, and he managed to make

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.460
a full recovery.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:27.670
Variety reported in January 2001 that production
was intended to resume, but the network axed

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.470
it after the schedule got a little too crowded.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.890
The pilot's cold open surfaced on YouTube
in 2006 and Schimmel posted some outtakes

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.380
to his Facebook page in August 2010.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.160
"Friend Me," announced in early 2012, was
to follow two friends who moved from Indiana

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.080
to Los Angeles to work at Groupon.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.620
How they ended up with that show title for
characters in that line of work remains a

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.620
mystery.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.620
Attached to star was Christopher Mintz-Plasse.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.540
Yeah, McLovin.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.450
“I am McLovin.”

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.210
Also attached was Nicholas Braun.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:01.400
He would insist on venturing out into their
new city while the other would remain a homebody

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.430
trying to keep his Midwest roots.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.370
Unfortunately, due to real life tragedy, it
never made it to air.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:11.520
"Friend Me" was co-created by Alan Kirschenbaum,
a veteran TV writer who was best known as

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.700
the creator of "Yes, Dear."

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.510
In October 2012, Kirschenbaum was found dead
in his home of a suspected suicide.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.990
This, among many other things, put the show
— which had yet to debut — in a state

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.340
of limbo.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.980
After months of delays and uncertainty, then-CBS
President Les Moonves confirmed at an August

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.810
2013 TCA press event that there were no plans
to show or burn off the episodes.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.230
As such, the show remains unaired to this
day.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.640
Few "Doctor Who" spinoffs were more enduring
than "The Sarah Jane Adventures."

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.370
Investigative journalist Sarah Jane Smith,
played by Elisabeth Sladen, was one of the

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.730
longest tenured characters on television.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:51.900
This made all the more impressive that she
came from a series marked entirely by change.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.070
"The Sarah Jane Adventures" was aimed at a
young audience and followed Sarah Jane on

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.960
her investigative alien journeys.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.380
“I saw amazing things out there in space.”

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.600
The show was a huge hit, and it was stopped
by the only thing that could have stopped

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.650
it: Sladen's death.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:13.260
Producer Brian Minchin told Doctor Who Magazine
in 2016 that the BBC loved the show and expected

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.890
to get more seasons after the current one
wrapped.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:20.510
Then in early 2011, Sladen called producer
Russell T. Davies and informed him that she

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.530
had cancer and didn't have long to live.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.820
The crew, over 100 people, kept it secret.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.120
Between the death of their star and the ensuing
heartbreak, all parties involved decided to

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.660
regretfully end the show.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.100
Minchin did have a curious note, though:

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.550
"Russell emailed me not so long ago saying,
'We shouldn't have stopped, should we?'

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.310
He thinks we canceled the show out of shock.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.760
And he's right.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.650
It was such a lovely show, and Lis was fantastic."

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:51.920
On April 19, 2020, the nine-year anniversary
of her death, Davies put together a final

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.710
episode: "Farewell, Sarah Jane," which depicted
a memorial service for the title character.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:02.570
"Grace Under Fire" was one of the first big
hits from TV megaproducer Chuck Lorre.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.920
The show starred Brett Butler as Grace, a
single mother raising her kids after a divorce.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.920
Grace was a recovering alcoholic, while Butler
herself was anything but recovered.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.210
This caused problems for her and everyone
around her, which ultimately ended the show.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:21.880
Butler became infamous for her volatile backstage
behavior, with a bad temperament and a drug

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.090
addiction that kept feeding off each other.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.430
She was known for berating her coworkers,
which led to five executive producers over

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.130
a mere five years and the departure of two
cast members.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.340
Entertainment Weekly reported that the low
point was "Vega$," a crossover event with

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.210
other popular sitcoms.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:42.480
Production teams for the other shows were
mortified by her behavior, which often delayed

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.480
production.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:47.230
On top of her erratic behavior were her trips
to rehab for painkiller addiction.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:52.240
Each visit stopped production, dropped ratings,
and just added to the frustration.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.130
One of these trips was partway through Season
5.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:59.680
Unable to handle Butler's erratic behavior
anymore, ABC shut down the show.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.800
According to the Orlando Sentinel, the production
company claimed,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.220
"We have decided to suspend production [...] so
that Brett may have time to resolve some personal

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.870
issues."

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.240
Production never resumed.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:17.030
"Megan Wants A Millionaire" was one of about
a billion D-list celebrity dating shows from

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.290
the late 2000s.

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:22.770
It starred former "Rock of Love" contestant
Megan Hauserman, who had a bunch of millionaires

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.730
compete to make her a trophy wife.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.790
But the show got canceled before the end of
the first season after one of the contestants

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.560
killed himself and someone else.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.960
The contestant in question was real estate
investor Ryan Jenkins.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.920
The entire series was in the can by the time
he committed his crime, so we know he came

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.100
in third.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:44.470
Not long after he wrapped his part in the
series, he married a woman named Jasmine Fiore,

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.540
only for the two to later separate.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:50.080
Jenkins joined the cast of "I Love Money"
season 3.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.270
Fiore's body, which the New York Times described
as "mutilated," was found in late April 2009,

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.520
and Jenkins fled to Canada after getting charged
with killing her.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.750
VH1 promptly canceled "Megan Wants A Millionaire"
and left the last few episodes unaired.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:09.260
Jenkins was later found dead in a motel in
Hope, British Columbia, of an apparent suicide.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:14.420
"I Love Money" season 3 was also preemptively
pulled, never airing an episode.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:19.590
"Ellen" was a hit sitcom in its time, but
it will always be remembered for one thing:

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.139
"The Puppy Episode."

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:25.150
It saw the main character Ellen, played by
Ellen DeGeneres, come out of the closet — not

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:27.660
long after DeGeneres herself did in real life.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:33.389
"The Puppy Episode" drew monster ratings and
got massive publicity, but it also marked

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:35.340
the beginning of the end for the show.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.960
Going forward, DeGeneres and ABC clashed behind
the scenes.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.180
The New York Times reported that ABC tried
to limit the number of LGBTQ storylines and

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:48.350
even at one time slapped a special viewer's
advisory in front of the show.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:53.520
DeGeneres was quick to point out that shows
about heterosexual relationships had no such

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:54.520
warning.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:59.910
GLAAD media director Chaz Bono noted to Variety
the dangers in the show becoming, quote, "too

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:03.430
LGBTQ,” especially after it became important
to the plots.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:04.430
He said,

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:06.380
"This is network prime time.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:12.160
When a show treats gay issues over and over
again, it becomes 'a gay show,' and the average

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.900
viewer says, 'Hey, I'm not gay — I'm not
going to watch it.'"

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.210
The show's ratings declined, and it's certainly
possible that the audience and networks weren't

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.449
quite ready.

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:26.270
If the show's cancellation wasn't proof enough
of this, the hit that Laura Dern's career

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.340
took was.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:32.180
Dern, whose character made Ellen realize that
she was gay, started getting death threats.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:37.099
Dern hit a career peak with "Jurassic Park,"
but the backlash to "The Puppy Episode" meant

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:42.130
that she stopped getting offered roles for
a significant period of time.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:47.139
In 2017, CBS aired a sequel series to the
classic Denzel Washington film "Training Day."

00:08:47.139 --> 00:08:50.970
The show was clearly meant to be a major cornerstone
for the network.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:55.959
The production team included Antoine Fuqua,
the director of the movie, and producer Jerry

00:08:55.959 --> 00:08:59.580
Bruckheimer, and had a legit star in Bill
Paxton.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:04.579
Nonetheless, the show only garnered modest
ratings — not exactly a smash hit.

00:09:04.579 --> 00:09:06.920
Then the show hit a bigger issue.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.759
After four episodes aired, Bill Paxton died
at the age of 61.

00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:14.620
All 13 episodes were in the can at the time,
so production wasn't impeded, but that was

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.860
a small consolation given the sudden death
of the well-liked star.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:22.119
Many sources, including Deadline, predicted
that Paxton's death guaranteed the show wouldn't

00:09:22.119 --> 00:09:23.629
get another season.

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:28.879
This proved accurate: there was a small uptick
in ratings after his death before they tanked.

00:09:28.879 --> 00:09:34.550
"Training Day" already had a weak path forward,
one that turned impossible without Paxton.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.749
The Duggar family — all 19 kids and counting
— made TLC a lot of money over the course

00:09:39.749 --> 00:09:40.970
of two shows.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:45.360
Both of these shows ended due to abrupt and
upsetting circumstances.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.449
The first show, "19 Kids and Counting," ran
on the network from 2008 to 2015.

00:09:50.449 --> 00:09:54.779
Viewers got to know the Duggar family, devout
Baptists who didn't use birth control and

00:09:54.779 --> 00:09:58.160
ended up with a massive, religious, homeschooled
clan.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.620
The show ended abruptly after it was revealed
that Josh Duggar, one of the sons, molested

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.410
five girls over several years — made more
sickening by the fact that four of them were

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:08.410
his own sisters.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.660
TLC went to the well again with the spinoff
"Counting On."

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:17.300
This show, which ran from 2015 to 2020, originally
followed some of the older Duggar siblings

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.399
before roping in the rest of the family.

00:10:19.399 --> 00:10:21.889
This, too, was canceled due to Josh Duggar.

00:10:21.889 --> 00:10:27.829
As if his previous actions weren't disgusting
enough, he now faces federal charges for receiving

00:10:27.829 --> 00:10:31.699
and possessing child sexually abusive material.

00:10:31.699 --> 00:10:36.329
Even though he wasn't part of the show, the
name and the publicity was too much for all

00:10:36.329 --> 00:10:40.690
involved — especially the network — to
deal with in public.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:45.189
"The Jeremy Kyle Show" was one of the longest-running
tabloid talk shows in the UK.

00:10:45.189 --> 00:10:50.199
For those unfamiliar, take a minute to imagine
"The Jerry Springer Show" for a class of people

00:10:50.199 --> 00:10:52.670
who call French fries "chips."

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:57.810
In typical tabloid talk fashion, things often
got controversial, violent, and embarrassing.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:03.230
A popular tool on the show was a lie detector,
and it was one such segment that led to the

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.880
show finally getting canceled.

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.569
During the first week of May 2019, a man named
Steve Dymond went on "The Jeremy Kyle Show"

00:11:09.569 --> 00:11:12.379
to prove that he hadn't cheated on his ex-partner.

00:11:12.379 --> 00:11:16.430
Dymond failed the lie detector test, and Kyle
mocked him.

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:20.339
Family and friends report that Dymond, who
was already diagnosed with depression and

00:11:20.339 --> 00:11:23.920
on medication, was distressed and humiliated.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.449
On May 9, Dymond was found dead in his home
of a morphine overdose, which many suspected

00:11:28.449 --> 00:11:29.709
to be a suicide.

00:11:29.709 --> 00:11:34.170
The ITV network put out a statement expressing
sympathies for Dymond's family and said,

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:38.490
"Given the gravity of recent events we have
decided to end production of 'The Jeremy Kyle

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:39.490
Show.'"

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:43.490
If you or anyone you know is having suicidal
thoughts, please call the National Suicide

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:50.189
Prevention Lifeline​ at​ 1-800-273-TALK
(8255)​.

